 or an uncle or a cousin or  something else  because basically anyone that lived in  the tri-state area if you lived in new  york jersey or connecticut you were  somehow  connected to somebody uh that died i had  a couple friends who's  had to move apartments and a whole bunch  of other stuff but that moment just that  that sort of simple human moment is what  sticks with me more than anything else  and uh and i hope i hope that in  these crazy times that we seem to live  in right now that maybe tomorrow  we'll be able to get back to a little  bit of what that feeling was like after  you know like i could sit here all day  long and tell you about the workstars  and and the crazy leftists and the  progressives and all that and i have all  my frustrations  but like if this project is to continue  this american project we're gonna have  to figure out a way  to to build some roads forward um  and there was a feeling after 9 11 that  that maybe we could there was  some national unity we don't have a lot  of it right now i don't know exactly how  we get back to it and i certainly hope  it doesn't take another you know tragedy  of that magnitude to get us there  um but that's that's uh that's my memory  that's my main memory of uh  it changed everything obviously i'd love  to to read some of your stories if you  guys want to throw some comments in the  comments section right down below  and that is the direct message for  september 10th  2020 and i will see you guys next week
 

